/** \addtogroup area_tree */
/** \defgroup theory_tree Theory of trees
 \ingroup area_tree

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/tree` realize the TREE and the binary
 tree of chapter 5 of the book, with pointers, with cursors, with lists of
 children and in vectors, and their visits; the binary search trees of
 chapter 6, plain, balanced by height (AVL), randomized (treaps),
 self-adjusting (splay trees) and threaded; the algorithms that build the
 sets and the maps on these trees by joins; and the B-trees of the external
 memory. This page collects the definitions and the notation that the
 headers share (see \ref theory_tree_notation), the representations of the
 trees with the costs of their operations (see \ref theory_tree_repr), the
 visits and the problems on binary trees (see \ref theory_tree_visits and
 \ref theory_tree_problems), the search trees and the arguments behind their
 bounds (see \ref theory_tree_search), the algorithms by joins (see
 \ref theory_tree_join) and the B-trees (see \ref theory_tree_btree). The
 pseudocode of each operation and of each algorithm is on the page of its
 header, in the comment of its function, together with the section of the
 book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_tree_notation Definitions and notation

 A <em>rooted tree</em> is a set of \f$ n \f$ nodes, empty or with one node,
 the <em>root</em>, and a partition of the others into subtrees whose roots
 are the <em>children</em> of the root; every node but the root has one
 <em>parent</em>, and the tree is <em>ordered</em> when the children of each
 node are in an order, the first being the leftmost (section 5.1 of the
 book). A <em>binary tree</em> is an ordered tree whose nodes have at most
 two children, told apart as the left and the right one even when only one
 is there. A node with no child is a <em>leaf</em>, the others are
 <em>internal</em>; the <em>depth</em> of a node is the number of edges from
 the root to it, the nodes of depth \f$ l \f$ form the <em>level</em>
 \f$ l \f$, and the <em>height</em> of a tree is the largest depth of its
 nodes, \f$ -1 \f$ for the empty tree.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$</td><td>the number of nodes, or of keys</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ h \f$</td><td>the height of a tree</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ T_1 , \ldots , T_k \f$</td><td>the subtrees of the children
     of a node, in their order</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ p[ i ] \f$</td><td>the parent of the node \f$ i \f$ in a
     vector of parents, -1 (<tt>NIL</tt>) at a root</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ d \f$</td><td>the degree of a complete \f$ d \f$-ary tree, or
     the number of children of a node</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{bh}( x ) \f$</td><td>the black height of the node
     \f$ x \f$ of a Red-Black tree, the black nodes on a path from
     \f$ x \f$ down to a leaf nil</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_tree_repr Representations and their costs

 <table>
 <caption>The representations of the ordered and of the binary trees and
          the cost of their operations (sections 5.2, 5.4 and 5.5 of the
          book)</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Representation</th><th>Parent</th>
     <th>First child, next sibling</th><th>Insert a child</th>
     <th>Space</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Tree</tt></td><td>pointers to the parent, the first child and
     the next sibling</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CursorTree</tt></td><td>the same links as indices in a vector,
     with a free list</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ChildListTree</tt></td><td>a list of the children per node,
     each node with its cell</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ParentTree</tt></td><td>a vector of parents</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>none</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, no order among
     the children</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, one integer per node</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>PreorderArrayTree</tt></td><td>the nodes in a vector in
     preorder, with their parents</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ and \f$ O( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BinaryTree</tt></td><td>pointers to the parent and to the two
     children</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ (the left and
     the right child)</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CursorBinaryTree</tt></td><td>the same links as indices in a
     vector</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ArrayBinaryTree</tt></td><td>a positional vector, the children
     of \f$ i \f$ in \f$ 2 i + 1 \f$ and \f$ 2 i + 2 \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>linear in the subtree
     copied</td><td>\f$ O( 2^h ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 The links to the first child and to the next sibling give every ordered
 tree, whatever the degrees of its nodes, three pointers per node, and
 they make it a binary tree: the left child of a node is its first child and
 the right child its next sibling (figure 5.9 of the book, `to_binary()` of
 `BinaryTree.h`), a correspondence one to one between the ordered forests
 and the binary trees. The vector of parents (section 5.4) needs one
 integer per node and answers the parent alone, which is what the trees of
 the visits of a graph and the forests of the MFSET need; in preorder, the
 same vector knows the children too, the subtree of a node being the
 stretch of places that starts with it and ends at the first place whose
 parent precedes it. A complete \f$ d \f$-ary tree needs no link at all:
 numbered by levels from 0, the children of the node \f$ i \f$ are
 \f[
   d i + 1 , \ldots , d i + d , \qquad \text{its parent}\
   \lfloor ( i - 1 ) / d \rfloor , \tag{1}
 \f]
 which is how a heap is stored (see \ref theory_priority); a
 binary tree far from complete wastes the places of the missing nodes, up
 to \f$ 2^{h+1} - 1 \f$ places for \f$ n = h + 1 \f$ nodes. The cursors put
 the nodes in one vector, so that the tree can be copied, saved or moved as
 a whole, and the indices stay valid when the vector grows.

 \section theory_tree_visits Visits

 A <em>visit</em> examines each node of a tree once: the
 <em>previsit</em> examines a node before its subtrees, the
 <em>postvisit</em> after them, the <em>invisit</em> with index \f$ i \f$
 after its first \f$ i \f$ subtrees (on a binary tree, with \f$ i = 1 \f$,
 the <em>symmetric</em> visit), and the visit <em>by levels</em> examines
 the nodes by increasing depth, by a queue (section 5.3).

 <em>Lemma 1 (the cost of a visit).</em> Each visit of a tree of \f$ n \f$
 nodes costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ time on the representations with the links
 to the first child and to the next sibling, or to the two children, and
 \f$ O( h ) \f$ space for its stack, explicit or of the calls.

 Indeed, each node is examined once, and each link is followed once from
 the node it leaves: the first child of a node when the node is entered, the
 next sibling when its subtree is over. The stack holds the nodes of the
 path from the root to the current node, i.e., at most \f$ h + 1 \f$ of
 them; the headers keep it explicit, since a tree of height \f$ n - 1 \f$
 would overflow the stack of the calls.

 The visits need no stack at all when the nodes have the link to their
 parent: the next node in symmetric order of a binary tree is the leftmost
 node of the right subtree, if there is one, and otherwise the lowest
 ancestor reached from a left child (`inorder()` of `BinaryTree.h`, and
 `PathIterator` of `Iterators.h`, which keeps the path instead of the links
 to the parents). A whole visit costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ even so, since
 each edge is walked once down and once up, while a single step costs
 \f$ O( h ) \f$. The <em>threads</em> of Perlis and Thornton reach the same
 end with no link to the parent: a missing left child is replaced by a link
 to the predecessor and a missing right child by a link to the successor,
 a bit of each node telling a child from a thread, so that the successor is
 the right thread or the leftmost node of the right subtree (`ThreadedTree`).
 The \f$ n + 1 \f$ links that a binary tree of \f$ n \f$ nodes leaves nil
 are exactly the threads.

 \section theory_tree_problems Problems on binary trees

 The functions of `BinaryTreeAlgorithms.h` work on any representation of
 the nodes with the links to the children, and to the parent where they need
 it. Two nodes \f$ u \f$ and \f$ v \f$ have their lowest common ancestor at
 the point where the paths from them to the root meet; once the deeper node
 has climbed to the depth of the other, the two climb together and meet
 there, i.e., \f$ O( h ) \f$ time and \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ space. A binary tree
 is a search tree if and only if each key lies in the open interval that its
 ancestors leave to it, which a previsit carrying the two ends checks in
 \f$ O( n ) \f$, while comparing each node with its children only is not
 enough. A search for the keys in \f$ [ a , b ] \f$ examines, beside the
 \f$ k \f$ keys reported, only the nodes on the two paths that search for
 \f$ a \f$ and for \f$ b \f$, i.e., \f$ O( h + k ) \f$ nodes. A sorted
 vector gives the search tree of least height,
 \f$ \lceil \log_2( n + 1 ) \rceil - 1 \f$, with the middle key at the root
 and the two halves below, in \f$ O( n ) \f$ by
 \f$ T( n ) = 2 T( n / 2 ) + O( 1 ) \f$.

 The previsit and the invisit of a binary tree of distinct values determine
 it, since the first value of the previsit is the root and splits the
 invisit into the two subtrees; a stack of the nodes whose right subtree is
 still open rebuilds it in \f$ O( n ) \f$, each node pushed and popped once.
 The <em>Cartesian tree</em> of a vector \f$ A \f$ (Vuillemin) has the
 leftmost minimum of \f$ A \f$ at its root and the Cartesian trees of the
 parts before and after it as its subtrees: it is the only binary tree whose
 invisit is \f$ A \f$ and which is a heap, and a stack of its right spine
 builds it in \f$ O( n ) \f$, since each item is pushed and popped once; the
 lowest common ancestor of two places in it is the minimum of \f$ A \f$
 between them, which ties the two problems together (Harel and Tarjan, and
 the range minimum of `RangeMinimum.h`).

 A heap of \f$ n \f$ items is built either by inserting the items one at a
 time, each climbing to its place (Williams, `HeapByInsertion.h`), or by
 sifting down the internal nodes from the last one to the root (Floyd). The
 insertions cost \f$ \Theta( n \log n ) \f$ in the worst case, an increasing
 vector, where each item climbs to the root, while the bottom-up
 construction costs \f$ O( n ) \f$: the \f$ \lceil n / 2^{l+1} \rceil \f$
 nodes of height \f$ l \f$ go down at most \f$ l \f$ levels each, and
 \f$ \sum_l l \, n / 2^{l+1} \le n \f$. On a random permutation the
 insertions cost \f$ O( n ) \f$ on average as well (Hayward and McDiarmid),
 and they are the method to use when the heap must be valid after each
 item.

